  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nToday through Friday will be defined by 500 mb troughing to the\nwest weakening and lifting bringing multiple shortwave troughs\nthrough the region. PWAT values are generally expected to be\nin the 100-120% of normal range with weak cold fronts and the\nshortwave troughs providing forcing. Todays shortwave will travel\nthrough Central Montana bringing a level 1 of 5 risk for severe \nstorms for locations west of Billings. The main threat with these \nstorms will be strong wind gusts in excess of 60 mph. Thursdays \nand Fridays shortwaves will impact Eastern Montana with a risk \nlevel 1 of 5 for severe storms east of Billings Thursday becoming \na level 2 of 5 near the Dakotas border. \n\nOver the weekend and through early next, most models are\nindicating a switch to more zonal flow and eventually upper level\nridging. This is expected to keep precipitation chances to a\nminimum and bring warmer temperatures. Starting Sunday, most of\nthe region is expected to see temperatures around 5 degrees above\naverage in the high 80s to low 90s F. Temperatures to start the\nwork week will likely remain in this range or increase slightly.\nModels are also indicating that next Monday/Tuesday will see an\nincrease in moisture with southeasterly winds. However, there\nare little indications that this will lead to precipitation, \nlikely due to a lack of forcing. Torgerson",
